+---
+import TenderHubLayout from "../../layouts/TenderHubLayout.astro";
+---
+
+<TenderHubLayout
+  title="Eastern Cape Government Tenders"
+  description="Tender opportunities across Eastern Cape provincial departments, Nelson Mandela Bay and Buffalo City metros, and rural district municipalities."
+  hubType="Province"
+  hubName="Eastern Cape"
+  contextLabel="the Eastern Cape"
+  lastUpdated="2026-05-14"
+>
+  <h2>About procurement in the Eastern Cape</h2>
+  <p>
+    The Eastern Cape combines two metropolitan economies — Nelson Mandela Bay (Gqeberha) and Buffalo City
+    (East London) — with one of the largest rural service-delivery footprints in the country. Provincial
+    procurement runs through departments coordinated by the Eastern Cape Provincial Treasury, with significant
+    public-works tenders tied to rural school infrastructure backlogs and the provincial road network.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>Key procuring entities</h2>
+
+  <h3>Provincial departments</h3>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>Eastern Cape Department of Public Works and Infrastructure</strong> — schools, clinics, government buildings, especially in rural districts.</li>
+    <li><strong>Eastern Cape Department of Transport</strong> — provincial road maintenance, fleet services, scholar transport.</li>
+    <li><strong>Eastern Cape Department of Health</strong> — district hospitals, equipment, security and catering services.</li>
+    <li><strong>Eastern Cape Department of Education</strong> — large school infrastructure pipeline, sanitation, furniture.</li>
+    <li><strong>Eastern Cape Department of Rural Development and Agrarian Reform</strong> — agri-infrastructure, fencing, irrigation.</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <h3>Municipalities</h3>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>Nelson Mandela Bay Metropolitan Municipality</strong> — water and sanitation upgrades (Nooitgedacht scheme), port-adjacent industrial infrastructure.</li>
+    <li><strong>Buffalo City Metropolitan Municipality</strong> — coastal infrastructure, refuse, electricity.</li>
+    <li>District municipalities such as <strong>OR Tambo, Alfred Nzo, Chris Hani, Sarah Baartman, Amathole, Joe Gqabi</strong>.</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <h2>Where Eastern Cape tenders are advertised</h2>
+  <p>
+    All provincial and municipal tenders appear on the
+    <a href="https://www.etenders.gov.za" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">eTenders Publication Portal</a>.
+    The Eastern Cape Provincial Treasury publishes annual procurement plans, and the two metros maintain
+    their own supplier portals alongside CSD records.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>What sets the Eastern Cape apart</h2>
+  <p>
+    Three factors stand out. First, the <strong>scale of the school-infrastructure programme</strong> — the
+    province has worked through a multi-year pipeline of replacing mud schools and adding ablution facilities,
+    sustaining steady demand for civil and building contractors. Second, the <strong>rural road network</strong>
+    — Transport tenders cover gravel road maintenance across vast districts. Third, the
+    <strong>automotive industrial corridor</strong> around Gqeberha and East London means industrial services,
+    security, and specialised construction tenders linked to OEM and supplier-park infrastructure.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>Bidding strategy</h2>
+  <ul>
+    <li>For rural infrastructure work, build partnerships with locally-based emerging contractors — many tenders carry locality and CIDB Grade-based preference goals.</li>
+    <li>Watch the Department of Education's procurement plan closely; school construction is the steadiest pipeline in the province.</li>
+    <li>Plan for travel — compulsory briefings can be deep in OR Tambo or Alfred Nzo district.</li>
+  </ul>
+</TenderHubLayout>
diff --git a/src/pages/tenders/education.astro a/src/pages/tenders/education.astro
