@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+---
+import TenderHubLayout from "../../layouts/TenderHubLayout.astro";
+---
+
+<TenderHubLayout
+  title="KwaZulu-Natal Government Tenders"
+  description="Tender opportunities across KZN provincial departments, eThekwini Metro, and district municipalities — including flood-recovery and reconstruction work."
+  hubType="Province"
+  hubName="KwaZulu-Natal"
+  contextLabel="KwaZulu-Natal"
+  lastUpdated="2026-05-14"
+>
+  <h2>About procurement in KwaZulu-Natal</h2>
+  <p>
+    KZN's procurement landscape is shaped by its geography, large rural footprint, and an ambitious
+    infrastructure programme that has been amplified by post-flood reconstruction. The province runs
+    procurement through provincial departments coordinated by the KZN Provincial Treasury, alongside
+    one metro, two large urban municipalities, and ten district municipalities each with their own
+    SCM units.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>Key procuring entities</h2>
+
+  <h3>Provincial departments</h3>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>KZN Department of Public Works and Infrastructure</strong> — schools, clinics, community halls, especially in rural districts.</li>
+    <li><strong>KZN Department of Transport</strong> — major road maintenance and bridge construction; deadlines often cluster July–November.</li>
+    <li><strong>KZN Department of Health</strong> — district hospitals, central facilities (Inkosi Albert Luthuli, King Edward VIII), pharmaceuticals.</li>
+    <li><strong>KZN Department of Education</strong> — school infrastructure, scholar transport, ICT.</li>
+    <li><strong>KZN Department of Human Settlements</strong> — housing delivery and reconstruction.</li>
+    <li><strong>KZN Department of Agriculture and Rural Development</strong> — agri-infrastructure, veterinary services.</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <h3>Municipalities</h3>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>eThekwini Metropolitan Municipality</strong> — multi-year water and sanitation tenders, port-adjacent infrastructure, ICT.</li>
+    <li><strong>Msunduzi (Pietermaritzburg)</strong> — capital city services, electrical, water.</li>
+    <li>District municipalities such as <strong>uMkhanyakude, Zululand, King Cetshwayo, uThukela, Harry Gwala</strong>, and the constituent local municipalities.</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <h2>Where KZN tenders are advertised</h2>
+  <p>
+    All provincial and municipal tenders appear on the
+    <a href="https://www.etenders.gov.za" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">eTenders Publication Portal</a>.
+    The KZN Provincial Treasury publishes annual procurement plans by end-March, and eThekwini Metro
+    runs its own supplier database registration alongside its CSD record.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>What sets KZN apart</h2>
+  <p>
+    Two factors dominate KZN procurement right now. First, the <strong>flood reconstruction pipeline</strong>:
+    following the 2022 and subsequent flooding events, sustained national grant funding has produced a
+    pipeline of road, bridge, water, and housing reconstruction tenders. Many are fast-tracked with shorter
+    response times. Second, the <strong>rural geography</strong>: compulsory briefing sessions are frequently
+    held at remote project sites — uMkhanyakude, Zululand, parts of Harry Gwala — so logistical planning
+    is part of bidding here.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>Bidding strategy</h2>
+  <ul>
+    <li>Download the Public Works and Transport procurement plans by April each year and build a 12-month rolling bid calendar.</li>
+    <li>Pre-position relationships with KZN-based consortia partners for rural infrastructure work.</li>
+    <li>Set up email alerts on eTenders with commodity codes and the "KwaZulu-Natal" province filter.</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <p>
+    Read our <a href="/blog/kzn-procurement-deadlines">KZN procurement deadlines guide</a> for the
+    detailed calendar approach.
+  </p>
+</TenderHubLayout>
