+
+<TenderHubLayout
+  title="Legal Services Tenders in South Africa"
+  description="Government legal services tender opportunities — panel appointments for litigation, conveyancing, labour and commercial advisory, and specialist counsel briefs."
+  hubType="Sector"
+  hubName="Legal"
+  contextLabel="legal services"
+  lastUpdated="2026-05-14"
+>
+  <h2>About legal-services procurement</h2>
+  <p>
+    Legal-services tenders are dominated by panel appointments: organs of state appoint panels of attorneys
+    for periods of typically three years, drawing briefs from the panel based on rotation and matter type.
+    Categories include general litigation, labour and employment, commercial and contractual advisory,
+    conveyancing, debt collection, and specialist areas such as competition, tax, and constitutional
+    litigation. Counsel briefs are typically governed by State Attorney rules under the Department of
+    Justice and Constitutional Development.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>Detailed sector hub — coming soon</h2>
+  <p>
+    We're building a detailed legal-services hub covering State Attorney panel processes, departmental
+    panel tenders, the Black Briefing Patterns policy and transformation requirements, fee tariffs, and the
+    practical bidding strategy for small and emerging firms looking to get onto government panels.
+    Join the waitlist below.
+  </p>
+
+  <p>
+    Current legal-services tenders and panel adverts are published on the
+    <a href="https://www.etenders.gov.za" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">eTenders Publication Portal</a>
+    and on departmental websites.
+  </p>
+</TenderHubLayout>
diff --git a/src/pages/tenders/limpopo.astro a/src/pages/tenders/limpopo.astro
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e39f1c6
--- /dev/null
+++ a/src/pages/tenders/limpopo.astro
@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+---
+import TenderHubLayout from "../../layouts/TenderHubLayout.astro";
+---
+
+<TenderHubLayout
+  title="Limpopo Government Tenders"
+  description="Tender opportunities across Limpopo provincial departments, district municipalities, and mining-adjacent infrastructure spend in the platinum belt."
+  hubType="Province"
+  hubName="Limpopo"
+  contextLabel="Limpopo"
+  lastUpdated="2026-05-14"
+>
+  <h2>About procurement in Limpopo</h2>
+  <p>
+    Limpopo's procurement environment is shaped by a large rural population, a substantial school and clinic
+    infrastructure backlog, and proximity to the platinum and chrome mining belt. The province has no metro;
+    five district municipalities and their constituent local municipalities run procurement alongside the
+    provincial departments coordinated by the Limpopo Provincial Treasury.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>Key procuring entities</h2>
+
+  <h3>Provincial departments</h3>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>Limpopo Department of Public Works, Roads and Infrastructure</strong> — combined function covering provincial buildings and the road network.</li>
+    <li><strong>Limpopo Department of Health</strong> — Polokwane Provincial Hospital, district hospitals, equipment.</li>
+    <li><strong>Limpopo Department of Education</strong> — large school infrastructure pipeline, sanitation, scholar transport.</li>
+    <li><strong>Limpopo Department of Agriculture and Rural Development</strong> — agri-infrastructure, veterinary services, fencing.</li>
+    <li><strong>Limpopo Department of Economic Development, Environment and Tourism</strong> — SEZ-related infrastructure (Musina-Makhado).</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <h3>Municipalities</h3>
+  <ul>
+    <li>District municipalities: <strong>Capricorn, Mopani, Sekhukhune, Vhembe, Waterberg</strong>.</li>
+    <li>Constituent locals include <strong>Polokwane, Tzaneen, Mookgophong–Modimolle, Musina, Thulamela</strong>, and others.</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <h2>Where Limpopo tenders are advertised</h2>
+  <p>
+    All provincial and municipal tenders appear on the
+    <a href="https://www.etenders.gov.za" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">eTenders Publication Portal</a>.
+    The Limpopo Provincial Treasury publishes annual procurement plans.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>What sets Limpopo apart</h2>
+  <p>
+    Two factors define Limpopo's bid environment. First, the <strong>school infrastructure pipeline</strong>
+    — the province has run sustained programmes to replace mud schools and add proper ablution facilities,
+    with hundreds of tenders going to civil and building contractors annually. Second, the
+    <strong>cross-border and mining-adjacent infrastructure</strong> — the Musina-Makhado SEZ and the platinum
+    belt around Mokopane and Burgersfort generate periodic tenders for water, energy, and services
+    infrastructure with locality-specific preference goals.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>Bidding strategy</h2>
+  <ul>
+    <li>For rural infrastructure work, register with local Department of Public Works district offices and attend their supplier development sessions.</li>
+    <li>Travel and logistics matter — compulsory briefings can be deep in Sekhukhune or Vhembe districts.</li>
+    <li>For mining-adjacent work, ensure your B-BBEE certificate is current and consortium arrangements with locally-owned firms are in place.</li>
+  </ul>
+</TenderHubLayout>
diff --git a/src/pages/tenders/mpumalanga.astro a/src/pages/tenders/mpumalanga.astro
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..2865891
--- /dev/null
+++ a/src/pages/tenders/mpumalanga.astro
@@ -0,0 +1,60 @@
+---
+import TenderHubLayout from "../../layouts/TenderHubLayout.astro";
+---
+
+<TenderHubLayout
+  title="Mpumalanga Government Tenders"
+  description="Tender opportunities across Mpumalanga provincial departments, three district municipalities, and energy-belt infrastructure spend around the Highveld."
+  hubType="Province"
+  hubName="Mpumalanga"
+  contextLabel="Mpumalanga"
+  lastUpdated="2026-05-14"
+>
+  <h2>About procurement in Mpumalanga</h2>
+  <p>
+    Mpumalanga's procurement environment is shaped by the Highveld coal-power complex, the Lowveld tourism
+    and agriculture sector, and three district municipalities covering everything from urban Emalahleni to
+    the rural areas bordering eSwatini and Mozambique. The province runs procurement through departments
+    coordinated by the Mpumalanga Provincial Treasury.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>Key procuring entities</h2>
+
+  <h3>Provincial departments</h3>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>Mpumalanga Department of Public Works, Roads and Transport</strong> — provincial roads, buildings, scholar transport.</li>
+    <li><strong>Mpumalanga Department of Health</strong> — Witbank, Rob Ferreira, and district hospitals.</li>
+    <li><strong>Mpumalanga Department of Education</strong> — school infrastructure and stationery.</li>
+    <li><strong>Mpumalanga Department of Agriculture, Rural Development, Land and Environmental Affairs</strong> — agri-infrastructure, fencing.</li>
+    <li><strong>Mpumalanga Department of Human Settlements</strong> — housing developments.</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <h3>Municipalities</h3>
+  <ul>
+    <li>District municipalities: <strong>Ehlanzeni, Gert Sibande, Nkangala</strong>.</li>
+    <li>Constituent locals include <strong>Mbombela (Nelspruit), Emalahleni (Witbank), Govan Mbeki (Secunda), Steve Tshwete (Middelburg), Bushbuckridge</strong>.</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <h2>Where Mpumalanga tenders are advertised</h2>
+  <p>
+    All provincial and municipal tenders appear on the
+    <a href="https://www.etenders.gov.za" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">eTenders Publication Portal</a>.
+    The Mpumalanga Provincial Treasury publishes annual procurement plans.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>What sets Mpumalanga apart</h2>
+  <p>
+    The province sits at the centre of South Africa's <strong>Just Energy Transition</strong> conversation.
+    The phase-down of coal-fired power stations is generating tenders for repurposing, decommissioning,
+    skills development, and renewable energy installations. The Lowveld's
+    <strong>tourism, agriculture, and forestry economies</strong> drive a different stream of tenders
+    around the Kruger National Park boundary and the Mbombela area.
+  </p>
+
+  <h2>Bidding strategy</h2>
+  <ul>
+    <li>For energy-transition adjacent work, ensure your CSD profile includes renewable energy and decommissioning service codes.</li>
+    <li>The mining and power-station industrial corridor offers significant subcontracting opportunities for SMMEs in services.</li>
+    <li>Watch the Mbombela and Govan Mbeki municipal pipelines — they have the largest revenue bases in the province.</li>
+  </ul>
+</TenderHubLayout>
